.\" ucc-cli.1 — man page for ucc-cli
.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
.TH UCC-CLI 1 "March 2026" "ucc-cli 0.2.0" "Unified Control Center"
.SH NAME
ucc-cli \- command-line interface for Unified Control Center
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ucc-cli
.I command
.RI [ sub-command ]
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ucc-cli
is a command-line client for the Unified Control Center daemon
.RB ( uccd ).
It communicates over the D-Bus system bus
.RB ( com.uniwill.uccd )
to query and control hardware on Uniwill/Clevo/Tong Fang laptops:
.IP \(bu 2
CPU/GPU performance profiles (built-in and user-created)
.IP \(bu 2
GPU overclocking (NVIDIA clock offsets, locked clocks, power limits)
.IP \(bu 2
cTGP (configurable Total Graphics Power) offset
.IP \(bu 2
ODM performance modes (firmware-level power profiles)
.IP \(bu 2
Fan curve management (CPU, GPU, pump)
.IP \(bu 2
Per-zone keyboard backlight control (colours, modes, brightness)
.IP \(bu 2
Display brightness, webcam toggle, Fn Lock
.IP \(bu 2
Battery charging profiles, priorities, and charge thresholds
.SS Architecture
.B uccd
is a system daemon that mediates all hardware access.
.B ucc-cli
sends D-Bus method calls to
.B uccd
and prints the results.
No root privileges are required for
.BR ucc-cli ;
the daemon applies the requested changes via its own elevated permissions.
.PP
User settings (custom profiles, fan curves, keyboard profiles, GPU OC profiles,
and the power-state\[->]profile map) are stored in
.IR ~/.config/uccrc ,
an INI file shared with
.B ucc-gui
and
.BR ucc-tray .
.B ucc-cli
reads this file to resolve custom profiles that may not be registered
with the daemon.
.SS Profile Hierarchy
A
.I profile
bundles the following settings into a single named configuration that can be
activated atomically:
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
CPU: governor, EPP, core count, turbo, frequency limits
.IP \(bu 2
Fan: which fan curve profile to use, lock fans together
.IP \(bu 2
Display: brightness, refresh rate
.IP \(bu 2
Webcam: enable/disable
.IP \(bu 2
ODM power limits: PL1 (sustained), PL2 (boost), PL4 (peak) in watts
.IP \(bu 2
GPU OC data: embedded overclocking profile with cTGP offset
.IP \(bu 2
ODM performance mode (e.g.\& quiet, balanced, performance)
.IP \(bu 2
Charging profile
.IP \(bu 2
Keyboard profile reference
.RE
.PP
.B Built-in profiles
ship with the daemon and cannot be modified.
.B Custom profiles
are created by the user in the GUI or by saving via
.BR "ucc-cli profile save" .
Both types are identified by a UUID.
.PP
The
.B state map
assigns a profile UUID to each power state
.RB ( power_ac , " power_bat" ),
enabling automatic profile switching when the laptop is plugged in
or on battery.
.PP
.B Fan profiles
and
.B keyboard profiles
are separate entities referenced from within a main profile.
.B GPU OC profiles
define overclocking parameters (clock offsets, locked clocks, power limits,
cTGP offset) and can be applied independently of main profiles.
.PP
The daemon must be running for any command to succeed.
Check with
.BR "systemctl status uccd" .
.\" ===================================================================
.SH COMMANDS
Most top-level commands accept short aliases shown in parentheses.
Sub-commands also accept common aliases:
.B list
may be written as
.BR ls ;
.B get
as
.BR show ;
.B set
as
.BR activate ;
.B delete
as
.B del
or
.BR rm .
.\" -------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS General
.TP
.B status
Print a full system dashboard covering every hardware subsystem:
system model, CPU/GPU model names, active profile, CPU and GPU
temperatures, frequencies, power draw, fan speeds, extended NVIDIA metrics
(if a discrete NVIDIA GPU is present), hardware toggles (brightness, webcam,
Fn Lock), and charging information.
.IP
When NVIDIA extended metrics are available, the GPU section additionally
shows GPU load, VRAM bus load, VRAM usage, P-state, core voltage, and clock
offsets.
.IP
Use
.B \-\-json
for machine-readable output (see
.B JSON OUTPUT
below).
.TP
.BR monitor " (" mon ") " \fR[\fB\-n\fR " " \fICOUNT\fR] " " [\fB\-i\fR " " \fISECS\fR]
Continuously print a compact one-line summary per sample:
.RS
.PP
.nf
CPU: 72°C  4800MHz   35.2W  Fan: 55%  |  GPU: 65°C  1950MHz   80.3W  Fan: 60%  Load: 42%  P0
.fi
.PP
Fields: CPU temperature, frequency, power, fan percentage; GPU temperature,
frequency, power, fan percentage.
If a discrete NVIDIA GPU is present, GPU compute load (%) and current P-state
are appended.
.TP
.BI \-n " COUNT"
Stop after
.I COUNT
samples.
A value of 0 (the default) runs indefinitely until interrupted.
.TP
.BI \-i " SECS"
Polling interval in seconds (default: 2).
.RE
.\" -------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS "Profile Management \fR(profile, prof)"
.TP
.B profile list \fR(\fBls\fR)
List all built-in and custom profiles with their UUIDs and names.
Custom profiles that are assigned to a power state in the state map
are annotated with the state labels (e.g.\& \fB[AC, WC]\fR).
.TP
.B profile get \fR(\fBshow\fR, \fBactive\fR)
Print the active profile in a human-readable summary.
The output covers every embedded section: CPU settings (governor, EPP,
online cores, turbo, frequency limits), fan settings (profile UUID,
same-speed), display (brightness, refresh rate), webcam,
ODM power limits (PL1/PL2/PL4), cTGP offset, ODM performance mode,
charging profile, and keyboard profile.
.TP
.B profile set \fIPROFILE_ID\fR \fR(\fBactivate\fR)
Activate the profile identified by
.IR PROFILE_ID .
The daemon applies the profile's CPU, fan, display, keyboard, and hardware
settings atomically.
.TP
.B profile defaults \fR(\fBdefault\fR)
Print all built-in profiles with detailed per-section summaries.
.TP
.B profile customs \fR(\fBcustom\fR)
Print all custom profiles with detailed per-section summaries.
.TP
.B profile apply \fIJSON\fR
Apply a complete profile from a JSON string without saving it.
Useful for testing profile configurations.
.TP
.B profile save \fIJSON\fR
Save a custom profile (creates or updates).
The JSON must be a complete profile object with an
.B id
field (UUID).
.TP
.B profile delete \fIPROFILE_ID\fR \fR(\fBdel\fR, \fBrm\fR)
Delete a custom profile by its UUID.
.\" -------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS "State Map \fR(statemap, state-map)"
The state map controls automatic profile switching when the power source
changes.
.TP
.B statemap get \fR(\fBshow\fR)
Print the current state map (power state \[->] profile UUID mapping) and
feature toggles (CPU settings, fan control, keyboard backlight, temperature
unit, charging profile/priority).
.TP
.B statemap set \fISTATE\fR \fIPROFILE_ID\fR
Assign a profile to a power state.
.I STATE
must be one of:
.RS
.TP 12
.B power_ac
Mains / AC adapter
.TP
.B power_bat
Battery
.RE
.\" -------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS "Fan Control \fR(fan)"
Fan profiles define temperature\[->]duty-percent curves for each fan channel.
A fan profile is a JSON object with curve arrays; each curve is a list of
.B {"temp": \fIn\fR, "speed": \fIn\fR}
points (temperature in °C, speed in %).
.TP
.B fan list \fR(\fBls\fR)
List available fan profiles with their UUIDs and fan profile names.
Both daemon built-in profiles and custom fan profiles from
.I ~/.config/uccrc
are shown.
Custom profiles assigned to a power state are annotated with state labels.
.TP
.B fan get \fIFAN_PROFILE_ID\fR \fR(\fBshow\fR)
Print the fan profile's curve data as a formatted table for each channel
(CPU, GPU, Pump).
Works with both built-in and custom fan profiles; custom profiles are
resolved from
.IR ~/.config/uccrc .
.TP
.B fan set \fIFAN_PROFILE_ID\fR \fR(\fBactivate\fR)
Activate a fan profile by its UUID.
The stored curve keys
.RB ( tableCPU ", " tableGPU ", " tablePump )
are remapped to the daemon's apply format
.RB ( cpu ", " gpu ", " pump )
and sent to the daemon.
Works with both built-in and custom fan profiles.
.TP
.B fan apply \fIJSON\fR
Apply fan curves directly from a JSON string.
The JSON must contain one or more of the keys:
.RS
.PP
.nf
{
  "cpu":            [ {"temp": 40, "speed": 20}, {"temp": 80, "speed": 100} ],
  "gpu":            [ {"temp": 40, "speed": 20}, {"temp": 80, "speed": 100} ],
  "pump":           [ {"temp": 40, "speed": 30}, {"temp": 80, "speed": 100} ]
}
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B fan revert
Revert fan curves to the profile stored in the daemon (undo manual
.B fan apply
or
.B fan set
changes).
.\" -------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS "GPU Info & Overclocking \fR(gpu)"
.TP
.B gpu \fR(\fBgpu info\fR)
Show comprehensive GPU information in several sections:
.RS
.PP
.B Sensors\fR:
temperature, core frequency, power draw, fan speed (% and RPM).
.PP
.B NVIDIA Extended Metrics
(shown only when a discrete NVIDIA GPU is detected):
GPU compute load (%), memory controller load (%), VRAM usage
(used/total MiB), VRAM frequency, current P-state (P0\-P15), core voltage
(mV), graphics clock offset (MHz), memory clock offset (MHz),
NVENC utilization (%), NVDEC utilization (%), performance limit reason.
.PP
.B NVIDIA Power Control\fR:
cTGP available (yes/no), maximum power limit (W), default power limit (W),
current cTGP offset (W).
.PP
.B NVIDIA OC State
(shown only when overclocking is available):
organized OC capability and limits summary for device 0: GPU name,
offset/locked-clock support, GPU clock range, power limit range/default/current,
and active P-state clock offsets.
.PP
.B iGPU\fR:
integrated GPU temperature, frequency, and power.
.RE
.TP
.B gpu profile list \fR(\fBls\fR)
List all GPU OC profiles.
Built-in profiles are fetched from the daemon; custom profiles are read
from the
.B customGpuProfiles
key in
.IR ~/.config/uccrc .
Each entry shows its UUID and display name.
.TP
.B gpu profile get \fIGPU_PROFILE_ID\fR \fR(\fBshow\fR)
Display the contents of a GPU OC profile:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B cTGP offset
(W) \(em additional power headroom for the GPU
.IP \(bu 2
.B Core clock offset
(MHz) \(em added to the base graphics clock
.IP \(bu 2
.B Memory clock offset
(MHz) \(em added to the base memory clock
.IP \(bu 2
.B GPU locked clocks
(min\-max MHz) \(em clamp GPU frequency to a fixed range
.IP \(bu 2
.B VRAM locked clocks
(min\-max MHz) \(em clamp VRAM frequency to a fixed range
.IP \(bu 2
.B Power limit
(W) \(em maximum board power for the GPU
.PP
The full raw JSON of the profile is also printed.
.RE
.TP
.B gpu profile set \fIGPU_PROFILE_ID\fR \fR(\fBactivate\fR, \fBapply\fR)
Apply a GPU OC profile.
The apply sequence:
.RS
.IP 1. 4
Resolve the profile JSON: tries daemon first; falls back to
.B customGpuProfiles
in
.IR ~/.config/uccrc .
.IP 2.
If the profile contains a
.B cTGPOffset
value and the hardware reports cTGP adjustment as supported,
.B setNVIDIAPowerOffset()
is called first to raise or lower the power envelope.
.IP 3.
The profile UUID is injected into the JSON as
.BR gpuProfileId .
.IP 4.
The complete profile is sent to the daemon via
.B ApplyNvidiaGpuOCProfile
(device index 0), which applies clock offsets, locked clocks, and
power limits atomically.
.RE
.TP
.B gpu profile reset \fR(\fBclear\fR)
Reset all NVIDIA GPU overclocking to factory defaults:
all clock offsets are zeroed, locked clocks are removed, and the power limit
is restored to the default value.
.TP
.B gpu oc-state \fR(\fBocstate\fR)
Print the current NVIDIA OC state for device 0 as pretty-printed JSON.
Returns the live clock offset values, locked-clock ranges, and power limit
as reported by the driver.
Only available when
.B getNvidiaOCAvailable()
returns true.
.TP
.B gpu auto-oc \fR[\fBcore\fR|\fBvram\fR|\fBboth\fR]
Start an automatic overclocking scan using adaptive binary search.
The algorithm iteratively tests clock offsets from 0 to the configured
maximum, using GPU temperature, clock droop, and throttle-reason
monitoring to find the highest stable offset.
.IP
A GPU-intensive workload (game, benchmark, gpu_burn, etc.) should be
running during the scan \(em the algorithm checks GPU utilization and
warns if it is too low.
The procedure takes approximately 2\(en5\ minutes per component.
.IP
If \fIcomponent\fR is omitted, both core and VRAM are scanned
sequentially (core first).
After a successful scan the discovered offsets remain applied.
.TP
.B gpu auto-oc stop \fR(\fBcancel\fR)
Cancel a running auto-OC scan.  The offsets are reset to zero.
.TP
.B gpu auto-oc status
Print whether an auto-OC scan is currently running.
.\" -------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS "ODM Performance Profile \fR(odm)"
ODM performance profiles are firmware-level power modes that affect the
overall thermal and power behaviour of the laptop (e.g.\& quiet, balanced,
performance, enthusiast).
Not all hardware supports ODM profiles; commands will report
\(lqnot available\(rq when unsupported.
.TP
.B odm list \fR(\fBls\fR)
List all available ODM performance profiles and indicate the currently
active one.
If invoked without sub-command, behaves as
.BR "odm list" .
.TP
.B odm get \fR(\fBshow\fR)
Print the name of the currently active ODM performance profile.
.TP
.B odm set \fIPROFILE\fR
Set the ODM performance profile to
.IR PROFILE .
The value must match one of the profile names returned by
.BR "odm list" .
.\" -------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS "Keyboard Backlight \fR(keyboard, kb)"
.TP
.B keyboard info \fR(\fBcaps\fR)
Print keyboard backlight capabilities: zone count, maximum brightness,
maximum per-channel RGB values, and available lighting modes.
.TP
.B keyboard get \fR(\fBshow\fR)
Print the current per-zone backlight state.
If all zones share the same settings, a single uniform summary is shown;
otherwise a per-zone table is printed.
.TP
.B keyboard set \fIJSON\fR
Set per-zone backlight states from a JSON array.
Each element describes one zone:
.RS
.PP
.nf
[
  { "mode": 0, "brightness": 128, "red": 255, "green": 0, "blue": 0 },
  ...
]
.fi
.PP
.B mode
values:
.TS
tab(|);
c c
n l.
Value|Mode
_
0|static \(em solid colour
1|breathe \(em fade in/out
2|colorful \(em cycle through colours
3|breathe-color \(em breathing colour cycle
.TE
.RE
.TP
.B keyboard color \fIR\fR \fIG\fR \fIB\fR [\fIBRIGHTNESS\fR]
Set a uniform colour across all keyboard zones.
.IR R ,
.IR G ,
.I B
are 0\-255.
.I BRIGHTNESS
defaults to 128.
The mode is set to
.B static
(0).
.TP
.B keyboard profiles \fR(\fBprofile-list\fR, \fBls\fR)
List custom keyboard profiles from
.I ~/.config/uccrc
with their UUIDs and names.
Profiles assigned to a power state are annotated with state labels.
.TP
.B keyboard activate \fIPROFILE_ID\fR \fR(\fBuse\fR)
Activate a custom keyboard profile by its UUID.
The profile's stored backlight JSON is sent to the daemon.
.\" -------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS "Hardware Controls"
.TP
.B brightness get \fR(\fIalias:\fR \fBbr get\fR)
Print the current display brightness (0\-100).
.TP
.B brightness set \fIVALUE\fR
Set display brightness (0\-100).
.TP
.B webcam get
Print webcam status:
.B enabled
or
.BR disabled .
.TP
.B webcam set \fIon\fR|\fIoff\fR
Enable or disable the webcam.
Accepted values:
.BR on ", " true ", " 1 ", " yes ", " enable ", " enabled
or
.BR off ", " false ", " 0 ", " no ", " disable ", " disabled .
.TP
.B fnlock get \fR(\fIalias:\fR \fBfn-lock\fR)
Print Fn Lock status:
.B on
or
.BR off .
.TP
.B fnlock set \fIon\fR|\fIoff\fR
Enable or disable Fn Lock (same boolean values as
.BR "webcam set" ).
.\" -------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS "Charging \fR(charging, charge)"
.TP
.B charging status
Print charging profile, priority, charge type, and charge thresholds.
Only shown if the hardware supports firmware-level charging control.
.TP
.B charging set-profile \fIDESCRIPTOR\fR
Set the firmware-level charging profile.
Available profiles depend on hardware (query with
.BR "charging status" ).
.TP
.B charging set-priority \fIDESCRIPTOR\fR
Set the USB-C PD charging priority (e.g.\&
.BR high_capacity ", " balanced ", " stationary ).
.TP
.B charging set-thresholds \fISTART\fR \fIEND\fR
Set the battery charge start and end thresholds (percent).
The battery will not begin charging until it drops below
.I START
and will stop at
.IR END .
.\" -------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS "System Information"
.TP
.B cpu
Show CPU information: core count, live temperature, frequency, power draw,
available scaling governors, available energy-performance preferences (EPPs),
and min/max frequency limits.
.TP
.B power-limits
Show ODM power limits as TDP values:
PL1 (sustained), PL2 (boost), PL4 (peak) in watts.
.\" ===================================================================
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Output machine-readable JSON instead of human-readable text.
Currently applies to the
.B status
command.
See
.B JSON OUTPUT
for the schema.
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-h
Print usage information and exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-version ", " \-v
Print version and exit.
.\" ===================================================================
.SH JSON OUTPUT
The
.B "status \-\-json"
command outputs a single JSON object.
Fields that are unavailable on the current hardware are omitted.
Extended NVIDIA GPU metrics (marked with \(dg) are included only when
the value is valid (>= 0 or non-empty).
.PP
.nf
{
  "connected":      bool,
  "powerState":     "power_ac" | "power_bat",
  "activeProfile":  { \fI...full profile object...\fR },
  "cpu": {
    "temperature":  int,     // °C
    "frequency":    int,     // MHz
    "power":        double,  // W
    "fanPercent":   int,     // 0\-100
    "fanRPM":       int
  },
  "gpu": {
    "temperature":      int,
    "frequency":        int,     // MHz
    "power":            double,  // W
    "fanPercent":       int,
    "fanRPM":           int,
    "computeUtilPct":   int,     // \(dg GPU compute load 0\-100
    "memoryUtilPct":    int,     // \(dg memory controller load
    "vramUsedMiB":      int,     // \(dg
    "vramTotalMiB":     int,     // \(dg
    "perfLimitReason":  string,  // \(dg throttle reason
    "encoderUtilPct":   int,     // \(dg NVENC
    "decoderUtilPct":   int,     // \(dg NVDEC
    "currentPstate":    int,     // \(dg 0\-15
    "grClockOffsetMHz": int,     // \(dg if non-zero
    "memClockOffsetMHz":int,     // \(dg if non-zero
    "vramFrequencyMHz": int,     // \(dg
    "coreVoltageMv":    int      // \(dg
  },
  "igpu": {
    "temperature":  int,
    "frequency":    int,
    "power":        double
  },
  "hardware": {
    "displayBrightness": int,
    "webcamEnabled":     bool,
    "fnLock":            bool
  },
  "charging": {                  // only if hardware supports it
    "profile":        string,
    "priority":       string,
    "type":           string,    // omitted if "Unknown" or "N/A"
    "startThreshold": int,       // only if thresholds available
    "endThreshold":   int
  }
}
.fi
.\" ===================================================================
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Command error: invalid arguments, unknown sub-command, or a D-Bus method
call returned failure.
.TP
.B 2
Cannot connect to the
.B uccd
daemon.
Ensure the daemon is running:
.BR "systemctl start uccd" .
.\" ===================================================================
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B ucc-cli
communicates with the
.B com.uniwill.uccd
D-Bus service on the system bus.
The daemon is typically managed via systemd:
.PP
.RS
.nf
systemctl start uccd
systemctl enable uccd
.fi
.RE
.PP
No environment variables are read.
The D-Bus service name, object path, and interface are compiled in as
.BR com.uniwill.uccd " / " /com/uniwill/uccd .
.\" ===================================================================
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/uccrc
INI-format user configuration file.
Stores:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B stateMap
\(em JSON object mapping power states to profile UUIDs
.IP \(bu 2
.B customProfiles
\(em JSON array of user-created main profiles
.IP \(bu 2
.B customFanProfiles
\(em JSON array of user-created fan curves
.IP \(bu 2
.B customKeyboardProfiles
\(em JSON array of user-created keyboard presets
.IP \(bu 2
.B customGpuProfiles
\(em JSON array of user-created GPU OC profiles
.RE
.IP
This file is shared with
.B ucc-gui
and
.BR ucc-tray .
Edits made in one tool are visible to the others.
.\" ===================================================================
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Dashboard & Monitoring
Show a full system dashboard:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ucc-cli status
.fi
.RE
.PP
Get full status as JSON (for scripting):
.PP
.RS
.nf
ucc-cli status --json
ucc-cli status --json | jq .gpu.computeUtilPct
ucc-cli status --json | jq '.gpu | {temp: .temperature, load: .computeUtilPct, vram: .vramUsedMiB}'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Monitor CPU/GPU every 1 second, 10 samples:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ucc-cli monitor -i 1 -n 10
.fi
.RE
.PP
Continuous monitoring at default 2-second interval:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ucc-cli mon
.fi
.RE
.SS Profiles & State Map
List and activate a profile:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ucc-cli profile list
ucc-cli profile set 01234567-abcd-ef01-2345-6789abcdef01
.fi
.RE
.PP
View the active profile's full settings:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ucc-cli profile get
.fi
.RE
.PP
Assign profiles to power states for automatic switching:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ucc-cli statemap set power_ac  <performance-profile-uuid>
ucc-cli statemap set power_bat <powersave-profile-uuid>
ucc-cli statemap get
.fi
.RE
.SS Fan Control
.PP
.RS
.nf
ucc-cli fan list
ucc-cli fan get <fan-profile-uuid>
ucc-cli fan set <fan-profile-uuid>
.fi
.RE
.PP
Apply custom fan curves directly:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ucc-cli fan apply '{"cpu":[{"temp":30,"speed":20},{"temp":70,"speed":80},\
{"temp":90,"speed":100}],"gpu":[{"temp":30,"speed":20},{"temp":75,\
"speed":90},{"temp":90,"speed":100}]}'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Revert to daemon-stored curves:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ucc-cli fan revert
.fi
.RE
.SS GPU Overclocking
Show extended GPU info including NVIDIA metrics:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ucc-cli gpu
.fi
.RE
.PP
List, inspect, and apply GPU OC profiles:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ucc-cli gpu profile list
ucc-cli gpu profile get <gpu-profile-uuid>
ucc-cli gpu profile set <gpu-profile-uuid>
.fi
.RE
.PP
Reset all GPU OC to factory defaults:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ucc-cli gpu profile reset
.fi
.RE
.PP
Query live OC state:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ucc-cli gpu oc-state
.fi
.RE
.PP
Auto-tune GPU overclocking (run a GPU-heavy workload first!):
.PP
.RS
.nf
ucc-cli gpu auto-oc             # scan core + VRAM
ucc-cli gpu auto-oc core        # core only
ucc-cli gpu auto-oc stop        # cancel
.fi
.RE
.SS ODM Performance Mode
.PP
.RS
.nf
ucc-cli odm list
ucc-cli odm set performance
.fi
.RE
.SS Keyboard
Set keyboard to solid red at full brightness:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ucc-cli keyboard color 255 0 0 255
.fi
.RE
.PP
Set per-zone colours (e.g.\& 4-zone keyboard):
.PP
.RS
.nf
ucc-cli keyboard set '[{"mode":0,"brightness":200,"red":255,"green":0,\
"blue":0},{"mode":0,"brightness":200,"red":0,"green":255,"blue":0},\
{"mode":0,"brightness":200,"red":0,"green":0,"blue":255},{"mode":0,\
"brightness":200,"red":255,"green":255,"blue":0}]'
.fi
.RE
.PP
List and activate a stored keyboard profile:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ucc-cli kb profiles
ucc-cli kb activate <keyboard-profile-uuid>
.fi
.RE
.SS Charging
.PP
.RS
.nf
ucc-cli charging status
ucc-cli charging set-thresholds 40 80
ucc-cli charging set-profile balanced
.fi
.RE
.\" ===================================================================
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR uccd (8),
